Makefile: keep GCC 14's stricter C diagnostics as warnings

GCC 14 rejects by default a number of constructs that earlier compilers
only warned about, and this 2015 code base uses all of them freely:

  -Wint-conversion, -Wimplicit-function-declaration,
  -Wincompatible-pointer-types, -Wreturn-mismatch

Building with a modern toolchain therefore dies repeatedly, in vendor
code paths this board never executes - e.g. 'return' with a value in a
void function, in serial_sprd.c, sysdump.c, cmd_download.c and friends.

Downgrade them for both the host tools and the target build. -fpermissive
covers the whole class (GCC 14 accepts it for C precisely for this kind
of migration); the explicit -Wno-error= entries document the ones this
board actually hits and keep working if -fpermissive is ever rejected.

This does not paper over real defects: where a diagnostic pointed at an
actual bug rather than legacy sloppiness, it is fixed properly in its own
patch (see the fdt_for_each_subnode() argument order fix).

Signed-off-by: crackerjacques <jack@supremeoverlordjabs.co>

diff --git a/Makefile b/Makefile
index 3fe39cd..8db32e7 100644
--- a/Makefile
+++ b/Makefile
@@ -265,6 +265,10 @@ HOSTCXX      = c++
 HOSTCFLAGS   = -Wall -Wstrict-prototypes -O2 -fomit-frame-pointer
 HOSTCXXFLAGS = -O2
 
+# GCC 14 rejects by default several things this 2015 tree does throughout.
+# Keep them as warnings; real bugs get fixed in their own patches.
+HOSTCFLAGS   += -fpermissive -Wno-error=return-mismatch -Wno-error=int-conversion -Wno-error=implicit-function-declaration -Wno-error=incompatible-pointer-types
+
 ifeq ($(HOSTOS),cygwin)
 HOSTCFLAGS	+= -ansi
 endif
@@ -577,6 +581,7 @@ endif
 
 KBUILD_CFLAGS += $(call cc-option,-fno-stack-protector)
 KBUILD_CFLAGS += $(call cc-option,-fno-delete-null-pointer-checks)
+KBUILD_CFLAGS += -fpermissive -Wno-error=return-mismatch -Wno-error=int-conversion -Wno-error=implicit-function-declaration -Wno-error=incompatible-pointer-types
 
 KBUILD_CFLAGS	+= -g
 # $(KBUILD_AFLAGS) sets -g, which causes gcc to pass a suitable -g<format>
